        "html": "<h2>What is Film Halation Effect</h2>"
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "cls": "rte",
        "style": {
         "--padding-block-start": "0px",
         "--padding-block-end": "0px",
         "--padding-inline-start": "0px",
         "--padding-inline-end": "0px",
         "--width": "fit-content",
         "--max-width": "var(--max-width--body-normal)"
        },
        "html": "<p>Halation is the soft red-orange glow that appears around bright areas in film footage. It's caused by light penetrating the film emulsion, reflecting off the backing, and exposing the surrounding frame. Halation gives footage an organic, warm, cinematic quality that digital sensors can't produce on their own. That's why editors add halation effects in post — it's one of the fastest ways to make digital footage look like film.</p>"
       }
